 "substitutedBy": null,
 "summary": "Establishes an additional certification classification for minority and women-owned businesses which are ineligible for certification due to exceeding the personal net worth of small business thresholds.",
 "title": "Relates to the certification classifications for businesses owned by minority group members or women",
 "vetoMessages": {
  "items": [
   {
    "billId": {
     "basePrintNo": "A10744",
     "basePrintNoStr": "A10744-2017",
     "printNo": "A10744",
     "session": 2017,
     "version": ""
    },
    "billPage": 0,
    "chapter": 0,
    "lineEnd": 0,
    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": "\n \n                         VETO MESSAGE - No. 345\n \nTO THE ASSEMBLY:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nAssembly Bill Number 10744-A, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT  to  amend  the executive law, in relation to certification\n      classifications for businesses owned by minority group members  or\n      women\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n \n  This  bill  would  amend  provisions  of the Executive Law prevent the\nState's Minority- and Women- Owned Business Enterprises  (\"MWBE\")  Divi-\nsion  from imposing personal net worth (\"PNW\") and business size thresh-\nolds to business owners seeking to become certified  to  participate  in\nthe  MWBE  program, if the business can demonstrate that it \"engages in,\nand significantly invests in, diversity practices.\"\n\n  U.S. Supreme Court precedent is clear: government programs that  clas-\nsify individuals on the basis of race, as does the MWBE program, must be\n\"narrowly  tailored  to remedy prior discrimination...\" City of Richmond\n                                                        ________________\nv. J.A. Croson Co., 488 U.S.  469,  507  (1989).  In  2010,  significant\n__________________\namendments  were made to the State's MWBE certification program (C. 175,\nL. 2010) based on a disparity study which identified  numerous  barriers\nthat  have  impaired  MWBE  access to state contracting.  The study made\nrecommendations to ensure New York's MWBE  program  was  legally  sound,\nincluding,  adding  limitations on the size of a business that may enter\nan MWBE program and instituting a personal net worth test  to  determine\nprogram eligibility. These restrictions were critical to ensure that the\nMWBE program was compliant with Supreme Court precedent.\n\n  PNW and business size, are among several factors that serve as a basis\nto  identify  whether  a MWBE may participate in the program and to meet\nthe  requisite  graduation  or  limiting  factor  requirements  of  such\nprograms.  Notably,  comparable  programs lacking a PNW requirement or a\ncorresponding limiting factor have been struck down as unconstitutional.\nSee, e.g. Builders Ass'n of Greater Chicago  v.  City  of  Chicago,  298\n__________________________________________________________________\nF.Supp.2d  725  (N.D.  Ill.  2003)  (enjoining the City's program as not\nnarrowly tailored because \"[t]here is no net worth  threshold.  A  third\ngeneration  Japanese-American from a wealthy family, and with a graduate\ndegree from MIT, qualifies (and an Iraqi immigrant does  not).\");  Korn-\n                                                                   _____\nhaas  Const.,  Inc. v. Oklahoma Dept. of Central Services, 140 F.Supp.2d\n_________________________________________________________\n1232 (W.D. O.K.2001)(enjoining Oklahoma program  because  \"the  Oklahoma\nAct  has  no  inherent  time  limit, and no provisions for disadvantaged\nminority-owned businesses to \"graduate\" from  preference  eligibility\").\nThis bill would unnecessarily expose the MWBE program to legal challenge\nbe  removing  an essential component of the program without replacing it\nwith a corresponding limiting factor.\n \n  The State's MWBE program has provided access  to  countless  entrepre-\nneurs  and  businesses  and  provided  a  level playing ground for state\ncontracts.   However, we must  always  look  for  ways  to  improve  and\nstrengthen  our  business  climate and make it reflective of our current\nworld. Given that enabling legislation for the MWBE program  is  due  to\nexpire  in 2019, and because the determination of PNW, or other limiting\n \nfactor, is an important component to the reauthorization of  the  entire\nMWBE program, discussions regarding this issue should be considered in a\ncomprehensive  manner  during  the  2019  legislative session. For these\nreasons, I am constrained to veto this bill.\n \n  The bill is disapproved.                    (signed) ANDREW M. CUOMO\n \n                               __________\n",
    "signedDate": null,
    "signer": "ANDREW M. CUOMO",
